abstract: >-
  SkeneGraph-Net is a network-analysis application for TEI/XML-encoded drama,
  modelled on the DraCor API and departing from it on four
  methodological axes.  (1) Three segmentation methods — stage directions
  (a new segment after every <move>), div3 (the scene level), and div2 (the
  act / macro-structure level) — instead of DraCor's single scene-based
  segmentation.  (2) Three network models spanning two orthogonal axes: the
  cast filter (whether play-level mute characters are admitted as nodes) and
  the edge rule (co-presence on stage versus co-speech within a segment),
  yielding stage co-presence, dialogic cast at play level, and dialogic cast
  at segment level.  (3) Optional aggregation of collective figures — the
  chorus and its subdivisions — into a single node, or their treatment as
  distinct nodes.  (4) A per-segment dynamic graph, exported as a
  Gephi-renderable GEXF with <spells>, alongside the static aggregate.  Each
  methodological choice is exposed explicitly in the accompanying dashboards
  to support data-assisted — rather than data-driven — philological reading.
keywords:
  - TEI
  - Greek drama
  - Aristophanes
  - network analysis
  - computational philology
  - digital humanities
  - co-presence networks
